%S A400912 1,1,2,3,6,10,18,32,57,101,179,318,565,1003,1781,3162,5615,9969,17701,
%T A400912 31429,55806,99087,175937,312389,554672,984862,1748699,3104950,
%U A400912 5513080,9788899,17380947,30861215,54796475,97295379,172755470,306740697,544641831,967053691,1717078617,3048805877,5413390622
%N A400912 Number of compositions of n into parts of the form Fibonacci(k) - 1, k >= 3.
%C A400912 The allowed parts are the positive terms of A000071.
%C A400912 a(n-1) is the number of new shoots at step n in this growth model: at step 1 there is one new shoot of length 1. At each later step every shoot grows by 1, and each shoot whose length becomes a Fibonacci number >= 2 starts a new shoot of length 1. The total number of shoots after step n is Sum_{j=0..n-1} a(j) = 1, 2, 4, 7, 13, 23, 41, 73, 130, 231, ...
%C A400912 The growth constant r = 1.7755773368673624927... is transcendental: x0 = 1/r satisfies Sum_{k>=3} x0^(Fibonacci(k)-1) = 1, and since the exponents grow geometrically, a theorem of Corvaja and Zannier shows this sum is transcendental at every algebraic x with 0 < x < 1; as the sum equals 1 at x0, x0 and hence r are transcendental.
%D A400912 P. Prusinkiewicz and A. Lindenmayer, The Algorithmic Beauty of Plants, Springer-Verlag, New York, 1990.
%H A400912 Karan Sharma and Dheeraj Sonkhla, <a href="/A400912/b400912.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>
%H A400912 P. Corvaja and U. Zannier, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1015594913393">Some new applications of the subspace theorem</a>, Compositio Math. 131 (2002), 319-340 (Corollary 5).
%F A400912 G.f.: 1/(1 - Sum_{k>=3} x^(Fibonacci(k)-1)).
%F A400912 a(n) = Sum_{k>=3, Fibonacci(k)-1 <= n} a(n - Fibonacci(k) + 1) for n >= 1, a(0) = 1.
%F A400912 a(n) ~ c * r^n, where r = 1.77557733686736249278... and c = 0.57528843134774862366... = 1/(x0*P'(x0)), with x0 = 1/r and P(x) = Sum_{k>=3} x^(Fibonacci(k)-1).
%e A400912 a(4) = 6 counts the compositions 4, 2+2, 2+1+1, 1+2+1, 1+1+2, 1+1+1+1.
%o A400912 (Python)
%o A400912 def A(N):
%o A400912     F = [1, 2]
%o A400912     while F[-1] - 1 <= N:
%o A400912         F.append(F[-1] + F[-2])
%o A400912     parts = [f - 1 for f in F[1:] if f - 1 <= N]
%o A400912     a = [1] + [0] * N
%o A400912     for n in range(1, N + 1):
%o A400912         a[n] = sum(a[n - p] for p in parts if p <= n)
%o A400912     return a
%o A400912 print(A(40))
%Y A400912 Cf. A000045, A000071, A076739 (compositions into Fibonacci numbers), A357451 (compositions into tribonacci numbers; agrees with this sequence for n <= 11).
%K A400912 nonn,easy,new
%O A400912 0,3
